<section aria-labelledby="two-pictures">
  <h2 id="two-pictures">Two pictures of the same solid</h2>
  <p>
    Cohen and Louie begin with a productive tension. A solid is made of nuclei and electrons whose Coulomb interactions define the microscopic problem. Yet many experiments are easier to explain with objects that do not appear in that bare inventory: holes, phonons, plasmons, excitons, polarons, magnons, and superconducting quasiparticles. The first picture tells us what the material is made of; the second tells us how a low-energy disturbance propagates through it.
  </p>
  <p>
    These are not competing ontologies. An elementary excitation is a coordinated change of the interacting ground state that behaves, over a useful range, as if it carried definite energy, momentum, charge, or spin. Its existence and lifetime are properties of the material and of the approximation used to describe it.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="excitations">
  <h2 id="excitations">Why elementary excitations are useful</h2>
  <p>
    A macroscopic classification such as metal, semiconductor, or insulator compresses many microscopic details into observable behavior. The excitation picture opens that compression. A metal has low-energy charged states at a Fermi surface; a semiconductor requires finite energy to create an electron–hole excitation; a lattice supports acoustic and optical modes; an interacting electron liquid can support a collective density oscillation. Naming the excitation identifies what can carry a perturbation through the material.
  </p>
  <p>
    The word <em>quasiparticle</em> should therefore be read operationally. It means that a complicated disturbance has a sufficiently sharp energy and sufficiently long lifetime to be tracked as a propagating entity. If its spectral weight spreads strongly or its lifetime becomes too short, the quasiparticle picture loses resolving power rather than the underlying many-body system becoming ill-defined.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="probes-dispersion">
  <h2 id="probes-dispersion">Probes and dispersion</h2>
  <p>
    A probe transfers energy and usually momentum, so the book organizes excitations by a dispersion relation. The curve does more than assign an energy: its slope controls propagation, its extrema create high state density, and intersections with the energy–momentum window of a probe decide which processes are allowed. Neutrons couple strongly to nuclei and spin, photons to charge and current, and added or removed electrons to the charged one-particle spectrum.
  </p>
  <p>
    This immediately establishes a boundary used throughout the Guide: the calculated object must match the probe. A ground-state density, a Kohn–Sham band, a phonon branch, a charged spectral function, and an optical absorption spectrum answer different questions even when their plots share an energy axis.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="interaction-language">
  <h2 id="interaction-language">Interactions become processes</h2>
  <p>
    The final move is from isolated excitations to processes. An electron may emit a phonon, a photon may create an electron–hole pair, or two electrons may exchange a virtual excitation. The diagrammatic sketches introduced here keep track of incoming and outgoing labels and make conservation laws visible before the book introduces formal Green functions in Chapter 13.
  </p>
  <p>
    Read the diagrams as structured terms in a model, not as tiny classical trajectories. A line represents propagation of a chosen excitation and a vertex represents a coupling. Whether the process dominates depends on its matrix element, available phase space, occupations, and competing channels—questions developed in later chapters.
  </p>
</section>
